h lung organoids  and here the group was using cells to  mimic in the lung and they could produce  definitive mezzo and pulmonary ended  IRMS and eventually they could produce  branching Airways and alveoli and in  fact they use this as a disease model by  which they could look at infections for  example of SimCity or virus so growing  the cells into a lung organoid also  enabled them to start to develop a  disease model and in fact start to look  at how viral infection of lung tissue  proceeds and then a final example of  this of 3d culture when looking at  organoid this is a gastric organ owed  here as well in this case this is also  groaning Maitre D' Altis as it is in the  previous slide here human iPS see cells  have been grown into an endoderm tissue  the steroids are then directed using  various growth factors to form tissues  that had gastric glands pit like  glandular domains and from this system  the researchers could identify novel  signaling domains in always looking at  the fundamental organization and the  biochem  history of the cells and they could also  look at infection of things like h  pylori as well which is a fundamental  cause of human ulceration the slide here  shows I'm taking other intestinal  organized looking about in 96 well  that's the procedure we've developed  here at cording to develop intestinal  organized it's done in a steroid plate  in a Maitre dell system and you can see  that in the illustrations at the bottom  there indicate that you can start to see  the correct cellular organization with  the correct staining allowing on you to  see a variety of different cell types  there so given these examples and given  that long history i talked about and  given the acceleration in terms of  technologies you could reasonably ask  the question if cell culture has been  around for over a century why now is the  field only transitioning to study cells  in three dimensions and the answers are  fourfold one of which is that there are  new advanced tools to make this easy and  simple and reproducible to do and in  fact shown there is a corning ultra low  attachments lloyd plate which makes it  very straightforward to develop some  farad cell culture technique but  accompanied with this has been  enhancement of the instrumentation you  can do better imaging shown here is  looking at it down a microscope because  there are automated high-throughput  imaging technologies widely available  now and that relates to the other side  of it shown on the third panel there  which is standard high-throughput  instrumentation robotics Bleen have been  applied to the study of 3d cell culture  and that's also accelerating it as a  means by which it can be used in  compound screening and compound drug  discovery and there's increasing  benefits of course in terms of  developing the cells in 3d for the use  in terms of research as well as  therapeutic benefit and the last slide I  do just want to just just talk a little  bit about some of the various techniques  used and the slide here shows the major  techniques  which cells are studied in 3d and one of  the early methods of development was a  hanging drop technique off a cover slip  over a well and in that drop of fluid  the self-assemble into a steroid I've  also talked about the low attachment  plates where most area metrically design  such that the cells will grow into a  steroid you can look at them by inducing  them into a fluid the fluid layer by  introducing magnetic levitation the  cells have a magnetic particle on board  they will come together under the  influence of a magnet but increasingly  there are the techniques coming in the  use of micro fluidics  the use of 3d bioprinting and then also  the influence of growing cells on  Biological hydrogels as well as  synthetic hydrogels as well enabling  tools are propelling this field forward  I've talked a little bit about  microtiter plates shown here is a  steroid microtiter plate it's 1536  which means it can